      "id": "c2758925-6604-4fda-80aa-0a1efe98f77b",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great example 👍 This code is **packed with real-world Generics concepts**. I’ll explain it **from top to bottom**, focusing on **why** each part exists and **what problem it solves**—not just what it does.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Big Picture – What is this program doing?\n\nThis program demonstrates **advanced Java Generics**, especially:\n\n* **Wildcard generics** (`?`, `? extends`)\n* **Generic static methods**\n* **Multiple upper bounds** (`T extends A & B`)\n* **Type-safe filtering (querying) of objects**\n\nIn simple terms:\n\n> We have **Students** (and subclasses like **LPAStudent**) and a **generic query system** that can filter objects based on field names like `\"Course\"` or `\"YearStarted\"`.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ `QueryItem` – The Core Contract (Interface)\n\n```java\npublic interface QueryItem {\n    boolean matchFieldValue(String fieldName, String value);\n}\n```\n\n### Why this exists\n\nThis interface defines **one capability**:\n\n> “This object can be queried by a field name and value.”\n\nAny class implementing `QueryItem` promises:\n\n* It knows **how to match its own fields**\n* It can be **filtered generically**\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ `Student` – Implements `QueryItem`\n\n```java\npublic class Student implements QueryItem\n```\n\n### Key points\n\n* `Student` has:\n\n  * `name`\n  * `course`\n  * `yearStarted`\n* Implements `matchFieldValue`\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic boolean matchFieldValue(String fieldName, String value) {\n    return switch(fieldName.toUpperCase()) {\n        case \"NAME\" -> name.equalsIgnoreCase(value);\n        case \"COURSE\" -> course.equalsIgnoreCase(value);\n        case \"YEARSTARTED\" -> yearStarted == Integer.parseInt(value);\n        default -> false;\n    };\n}\n```\n\n### Why this is powerful\n\n* Query logic is **inside the object**\n* `QueryList` does **not need to know Student’s fields**\n* This follows **OOP + Open/Closed Principle**\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ `LPAStudent` – Subclass of `Student`\n\n```java\npublic class LPAStudent extends Student\n```\n\n### What it adds\n\n* Extra field: `percentComplete`\n* Custom `toString()`\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic String toString() {\n    return \"%s %8.1f%%\".formatted(super.toString(), percentComplete);\n}\n```\n\n### Important takeaway\n\nBecause `LPAStudent`:\n\n* **extends Student**\n* **inherits QueryItem behavior**\n\n…it works **everywhere Student works** without changing generic code.\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ `Employee` – Also Implements `QueryItem`\n\n```java\nrecord Employee(String name) implements QueryItem\n```\n\n### Why this exists\n\nThis shows that:\n\n> `QueryItem` is **not tied to Student**\n\nBut later you’ll see **why Employee is intentionally blocked** from `QueryList`.\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ `QueryList<T extends Student & QueryItem>` – 🔥 Multiple Upper Bounds\n\n```java\npublic class QueryList<T extends Student & QueryItem>\n```\n\n### This line is VERY IMPORTANT\n\nIt means:\n\n> T must be:\n>\n> * a `Student`\n> * AND implement `QueryItem`\n\n### Why both are needed\n\n* `Student` → guarantees student-specific behavior\n* `QueryItem` → guarantees `matchFieldValue(...)`\n\n💡 Java allows **only one class**, but **multiple interfaces** in bounds.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Instance Method – `getMatches(...)`\n\n```java\npublic List<T> getMatches(String field, String value)\n```\n\n### How it works\n\n* Loops through stored items\n* Uses `matchFieldValue`\n* Returns only matching items\n\n### Why this is type-safe\n\nIf `QueryList<LPAStudent>` is used:\n\n* Return type is `List<LPAStudent>`\n* No casting required\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Static Generic Method – 🔥 Independent of Class Type\n\n```java\npublic static <S extends QueryItem> List<S> getMatches(List<S> items,\n                                                       String field,\n                                                       String value)\n```\n\n### Why static method is different\n\n* Does **not depend on `QueryList<T>`**\n* Works with **ANY type implementing `QueryItem`**\n\nExample usage:\n\n```java\nQueryList.getMatches(students, \"YearStarted\", \"2021\");\n```\n\n### Key difference\n\n| Method type | Works on                   |\n| ----------- | -------------------------- |\n| Instance    | Only `Student & QueryItem` |\n| Static      | Any `QueryItem`            |\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ `printMoreLists(List<? extends Student>)` – Upper Bounded Wildcard\n\n```java\npublic static void printMoreLists(List<? extends Student> students)\n```\n\n### Why `? extends Student`\n\nAllows:\n\n* `List<Student>`\n* `List<LPAStudent>`\n\n### Rule to remember\n\n> **You can read, but you cannot add**\n\n```java\nstudents.add(new Student()); // ❌ NOT allowed\n```\n\nBut reading is safe:\n\n```java\nfor (var student : students) {\n    System.out.println(student);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔟 `testList(List<?>)` – Unbounded Wildcard\n\n```java\npublic static void testList(List<?> list)\n```\n\n### Why `List<?>`\n\n* Accepts **any list**\n* Replaces **method overloading** that would fail due to type erasure\n\n```java\ntestList(List<String>)\ntestList(List<Integer>)\n```\n\nThese two methods **cannot coexist** ❌ (type erasure)\n\n### Smart use of pattern matching\n\n```java\nif (element instanceof String s) {\n    ...\n} else if (element instanceof Integer i) {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣1️⃣ Main Method – Putting It All Together\n\n### Querying LPA students\n\n```java\nvar queryList = new QueryList<>(lpaStudents);\nvar matches = queryList.getMatches(\"Course\", \"Python\");\n```\n\n✔ Returns `List<LPAStudent>`\n\n---\n\n### Static querying students\n\n```java\nvar students2021 =\n    QueryList.getMatches(students, \"YearStarted\", \"2021\");\n```\n\n✔ Works without creating `QueryList`\n\n---\n\n### Why this line fails (commented)\n\n```java\n// QueryList<Employee> employeeList = new QueryList<>();\n```\n\n❌ Because:\n\n```java\nEmployee does NOT extend Student\n```\n\nEven though it implements `QueryItem`.\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Key Concepts You Learned from This Code\n\n| Concept                 | Where                           |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| Multiple upper bounds   | `T extends Student & QueryItem` |\n| Static generic methods  | `getMatches(...)`               |\n| Wildcards (`extends`)   | `printMoreLists`                |\n| Unbounded wildcard      | `testList(List<?>)`             |\n| Type erasure workaround | wildcard + instanceof           |\n| OOP + Generics design   | QueryItem pattern               |\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Mental Model (Easy to Remember)\n\n* **Interface (`QueryItem`)** → *What can be queried*\n* **Class (`Student`)** → *What data exists*\n* **Generics (`QueryList`)** → *Who is allowed*\n* **Wildcards (`? extends`)** → *Flexibility without mutation*\n\n---\n\nIf you want next:\n\n* 📊 **Flow diagram of QueryList**\n* 🧪 **Rewrite this using Streams**\n* ❌ **Common interview traps from this code**\n* 🆚 **`extends` vs `super` with real examples**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
